{"id":"edaf9a8d-d67c-4e0e-8452-a37b111581d5","slug":"falcon-9-block-5-sirius-sxm-8","flight":139,"name":"Falcon 9 Block 5 | Sirius SXM-8","mission":"SXM-8","net":"2021-06-06T04:26:00Z","precision":"minute","outcome":"success","status":"Launch Successful","vehicle":"Falcon 9 Block 5","vehicleSlug":"falcon-9-block-5","family":"falcon-9","pad":"Space Launch Complex 40","site":"Cape Canaveral SFS","orbit":"GTO","missionType":"Communications","crewed":false,"landings":{"attempted":1,"landed":1},"reused":true,"thumb":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/255bauto255d__image_thumbnail_20240305192900.jpeg","patch":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/mission_patch_images/spacex2520sxm-_mission_patch_20210606022236.png","description":"SXM-8 is a large high power broadcasting satellite for SiriusXM's digital audio radio service (DARS).\r\n\r\nSpace Systems/Loral (SS/L) got in July 2016 the contract to build the two satellites based on their SSL-1300 bus - SXM-7 and SXM-8. Satellite design operates in the S-band spectrum. Each satellite will generate more than 20-kW of power and will have a large unfurlable antenna reflector, which enables broadcast to radios without the need for large dish-type antennas on the ground.\r\n\r\nSXM-8 is meant to replace the XM-4 satellite. SXM-7 was meant to replace the XM-3, but suffered a failure in orbit 6 weeks after its launch.","image":{"url":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/falcon2520925_image_20210605162047.jpeg","thumb":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/255bauto255d__image_thumbnail_20240305192900.jpeg","credit":null,"license":null,"generic":true},"patches":[{"name":"SpaceX SXM-8","url":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/mission_patch_images/spacex2520sxm-_mission_patch_20210606022236.png"}],"window":{"start":"2021-06-06T04:26:00Z","end":"2021-06-06T06:25:00Z"},"probability":70,"weather":nu